WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
SSH trust is not only about proving the user to the server.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.880
It is also about helping the client recognize the server.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.020
Host keys support that decision.

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.440
When the client has a trusted record for the server, it can detect when the presented host identity is unexpected.

5
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:23.720
In interactive use, a person may see a host key prompt or warning and decide what to do.

6
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.100
In automation, there may be no human present.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.400
The job either proceeds, fails, or follows whatever trust behavior was scripted.

8
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:39.060
That makes known-hosts management a core part of secure SSH automation.

9
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.180
Secure Bash automation should avoid casual trust bypasses.

10
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:51.360
Disabling verification may make a job easier to run, especially in a temporary environment, but it weakens the trust boundary the automation depends on.

11
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.780
A remote deployment job should not have to guess whether it reached the expected server.

12
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.740
Managed trust can take several operational forms.

13
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:09.520
Teams may provision known host entries, maintain host key inventories, separate environment-specific host trust, and document how host key rotation is handled.

14
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:16.820
The exact process can vary, but the trust decision should be deliberate rather than an accidental side effect of whoever ran the script first.

15
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.700
Host key changes should be treated as events that require review.

16
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.120
Some changes are normal, such as planned rebuilds or rotations.

17
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.940
Others may indicate a misroute, DNS issue, environment mix-up, or unauthorized system change.

18
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.360
Automation should make those events visible instead of smoothing them over silently.
